ow. Not enough. Need four melds. So maybe this approach isn't working.\n\nWait, maybe I need to form another chow. For example, if I have T2 and T5, but no other tiles. Not helpful. Maybe there's another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should aim for a concealed hand with All Chows. But I need four chows and a pair. Let's see:\n\nIf I can form:\n\n- B4-B5-B6 (chow)\n\n- T2-T5-T7 (no, not consecutive)\n\n- W2-W2-W2 (no, only two)\n\nNot possible. So maybe this is not the way.\n\nAnother thought: since I have F3x2 and F4, maybe discard F4 to keep F3 as a pair. If I can form a pung of F3 later, but need a third one. But that's uncertain.\n\nAlternatively, discard T7. T7 has 3 left. Not too bad. If I discard T7, maybe I can focus on other suits.\n\nBut I'm not seeing a clear path. Let's consider the valid actions again. The options are to play F4, T7, B5, B8, W2, B6, T5, T2, F3, B4.\n\nWhich of these would help in forming a winning hand? Let's think about possible discards:\n\n- Playing B4 allows forming a chow in B suit. Even if not enough for a full hand, it's a step towards it.\n\n- Playing F4 might be safe due to low count.\n\n- Playing T7 could be safe since there are 3 left.\n\nBut I need to maximize the chances. Since I have B4x2, B5, B6, forming a chow is possible. So playing B4 to keep one B4 and form the chow. Then, the remaining tiles can be arranged into other melds. For example, if I have W2x2 as a pair, T7x2 as a pair, F3x2 as a pair. That's three pairs and one chow. Not enough. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard T2. If I discard T2, then I can focus on other suits. But not sure.\n\nWait, maybe I'm missing something. The hand has W2x2, T7x2, B4x2, F3x2. These are all pairs. If I can form two pungs and two chows. For example:\n\n- Pung of W2 (if I have three, but I only have two).\n\n- Pung of B4 (if I have three, but I have two).\n\nNot possible.\n\nAnother approach: since the hand is concealed, maybe aim for a Seven Pairs. I have W2x2, T7x2, B4x